<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One, Two, Three Bananas! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One, Two, Three Bananas!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Organize information from discussions into written form.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences about shopping (e.g., "I want bananas."). Can organize information from discussions about shopping into written form (e.g., "I need apples and oranges."). Can write simple phrases and sentences about shopping (e.g., "Three apples, two bananas.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of fruits (apple, banana, orange, pineapple) and numbers (one, two, three, four, five, ten). Students chorally repeat the names of the fruits and numbers. The teacher points to a flashcard and asks, "What is this?" and "How many?" (e.g., "banana," "three"). Students respond with single words or short phrases. This connects to previous lessons on vocabulary and numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, e.g., three apples. The teacher says aloud, "I want three apples, please." and then writes the sentence clearly on the board. The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in "apple" and "pineapple" and /b/ in "banana." The teacher then points to another flashcard, e.g., five bananas, and says, "I want five bananas, please." and writes it. The teacher emphasizes politeness and clear quantity expression, blending spoken phonemes to form words like /ˈm-ʌ-n-i/ ("money").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I say 'three apple' or 'three apples'?" (Expected answer: "Three apples!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I say 'I want three apples' or 'Three apples I want'?" (Expected answer: "I want three apples!")</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher provides examples for clarification, e.g., showing a picture of one apple and three apples, reinforcing the plural form.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated shopping list (written) and a short dialogue about shopping. The list includes items like "3 apples", "2 bananas", "1 pineapple".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret simple written messages and follow written instructions related to everyday tasks like shopping. Recognize common vocabulary related to the market or store.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large printout of a shopping list on the board. The list says: "Shopping List: 3 apples, 2 bananas, 1 orange, 5 pineapples."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the list aloud clearly, pointing to each item. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then shows a simple dialogue:
                            <br><i>Child: "Mom, I want a snack."
                            <br>Mom: "Okay, let's go shopping at the store. What do you need?"
                            <br>Child: "I need three apples and two bananas, please!"
                            <br>Mom: "Alright, let's buy them."</i>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Where are they going?" (Expected answer: "To the store/market.") "What does the child want?" (Expected answer: "Apples and bananas.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher says, "The child wants ten oranges." Students say "False!" or "No!". The teacher says, "They need two bananas." Students say "True!" or "Yes!".</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (writing prep):</b> The teacher distributes a handout with simple sentences like "I need ____ bananas." and pictures of fruits. Students match the picture to complete the sentence by writing the correct number or fruit name.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing simple sentences using "I want/need + number + noun" and target vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, store, market, money, buy, fresh, one, two, three, four, five, ten). Focus on correct spelling and sentence structure at A1.1 level.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences that have missing words. Students complete the sentences by writing the correct number or fruit noun, referring to a word bank with pictures if needed.
                            <br><i>Examples:</i>
                            <br><i>a) I want ______ apples. (Picture of 4 apples)</i>
                            <br><i>b) I need two ______. (Picture of bananas)</i>
                            <br><i>c) Can I have one ______, please? (Picture of orange)</i>
                            <br><i>d) Let's go to the ______. (Picture of market)</i>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Sentence Scramble & Rewriting:</b> The teacher writes scrambled words on the board or provides a worksheet. Students unscramble the words to form correct sentences about shopping and write them down.
                            <br><i>Examples:</i>
                            <br><i>a) three / I / want / bananas. -> I want three bananas.</i>
                            <br><i>b) buy / ten / Let's / pineapples. -> Let's buy ten pineapples.</i>
                            <br><i>c) a / need / basket. / I -> I need a basket.</i>
                            <br>This activity reinforces sentence structure for writing.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Writing & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students create a very short shopping list of 3-4 items they want to buy. Student A writes their list (e.g., "1. I want four apples. 2. I need two oranges.") and Student B writes their list. Then, they exchange lists. Each student reads their partner's list and checks for correct numbers, fruit names, and sentence structure. They politely point out one error if they find one (e.g., "Maybe 'five bananas' not 'five banana'"). Teacher circulates to monitor and assist with spelling and grammar.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product – Students compose a short text (a shopping list or a request note).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"My Shopping Note":</b> Students imagine they are going to the market or store. They need to write a note to a family member or friend telling them what items they want or need to buy.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a blank "shopping note" template with spaces for 3-5 items. The template could have prompts like:
                            <br><i>Dear ______,</i>
                            <br><i>Please buy:</i>
                            <br><i>1. I need ______.</i>
                            <br><i>2. I want ______.</i>
                            <br><i>3. Can you get ______?</i>
                            <br><i>Thank you!</i>
                            <br><i>Love, [Your Name]</i>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use the target vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, market, store, basket, price, buy, need, ask) and grammar (number + noun, "I want/need/can I have...") to write their personalized shopping note. They focus on composing simple phrases and sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages them to use polite expressions like "please" and "thank you." Students can draw a small picture next to each item if they wish. The focus is on the written output, organizing information, and expressing quantity clearly.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> The teacher collects the "My Shopping Note" papers and uses an observation checklist to assess students' written work. Focus on whether they can compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences about shopping.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "My Shopping Note."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., apple, banana, store, buy).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly uses number + noun (e.g., "three apples").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Composes simple, clear sentences (e.g., "I need two oranges.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes polite expressions (e.g., "please", "thank you").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Organizes information into a coherent note/list format.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you wrote today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write your shopping note?" (Students can draw a happy/sad face or circle a word.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I do well in writing today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about shopping do I want to practice more?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to support writing skills? (e.g., clear modeling, scaffolding of sentence construction, the shopping note template).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with sentence formation or vocabulary recall in writing? (e.g., more sentence starters, visual dictionaries, individual conferencing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their understanding of shopping vocabulary and expressions to complete a small project, possibly involving creating a menu or designing a simple "store" and explaining prices and items orally and in writing. They will mediate information from this lesson's shopping lists into a broader context.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>